[2026-10-29T08:06:37.730Z] [DEBUG] [rpc] github→tools/call 167b {"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"tools/call","params":{"arguments":{"issue_number":"5","method":"get","owner":"Kineth-t","repo":"pytorch-triage-agent"},"name":"issue_read"}}
[2026-10-29T08:06:38.470Z] [DEBUG] [rpc] github←resp 11553b {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"result":{"_meta":{"io.modelcontextprotocol/serverInfo":{"icons":[{"mimeType":"image/png","src":"data:image/png;base64,","theme":"light"},{"mimeType":"image/png","src":"data:image/png;base64,","theme":"dark"}],"name":"github-mcp-server","title":"GitHub MCP Server","version":"v2.0.3"}},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"issue\":{\"assignees\":[],\"author_association\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"### 🐛 Describe the bug\\n\\nOn MPS, a binary op between an MPS tensor and a **0-dim CPU float tensor with a non-zero storage offset** silently uses the value at storage offset 0 instead of the actual value. No error, no warning — just a wrong number.\\n\\n`coeffs[i]` for `i \\u003e 0` is exactly this kind of tensor, so any code that keeps a table of coefficients on the CPU and scales an MPS tensor by one of them gets silently wrong arithmetic.\\n\\n```python\\nimport torch\\n\\nmps = torch.full((2, 2), 4.0, device=\\\"mps\\\")\\ncoeffs = torch.tensor([9.0, 2.0, 7.0])          # plain CPU tensor\\n\\nfor i in range(3):\\n    s = coeffs[i]                                # 0-dim CPU view, storage_offset == i\\n    got = (mps * s).flatten()[0].item()\\n    print(f\\\"coeffs[{i}] = {float(s)}  offset={s.storage_offset()}  \\\"\\n          f\\\"mps * coeffs[{i}] -\\u003e {got}  expected {4.0 * float(s)}\\\")\\n```\\n\\n```\\ncoeffs[0] = 9.0  offset=0  mps * coeffs[0] -\\u003e 36.0  expected 36.0\\ncoeffs[1] = 2.0  offset=1  mps * coeffs[1] -\\u003e 36.0  expected 8.0     \\u003c-- wrong\\ncoeffs[2] = 7.0  offset=2  mps * coeffs[2] -\\u003e 36.0  expected 28.0    \\u003c-- wrong\\n```\\n\\nEvery result uses `coeffs[0]`.\\n\\n**Scope**, all measured on the same machine:\\n\\n| case | result |\\n|---|---|\\n| 0-dim CPU, `storage_offset == 0` | correct |\\n| 0-dim CPU, `storage_offset != 0`, **float32 / float16** | **silently wrong** (reads offset 0) |\\n| 0-dim CPU, `storage_offset != 0`, int32 / int64 | correct |\\n| `coeffs[1].clone()` (offset reset to 0) | correct |\\n| 1-dim CPU tensor, e.g. `coeffs[1:2]` | correctly raises `RuntimeError: Expected all tensors to be on the same device` |\\n\\nAll binary ops are affected, in both operand orders:\\n\\n```\\nmps * s  -\\u003e  36.000  expected  8.000\\ns * mps  -\\u003e  36.000  expected  8.000\\nmps / s  -\\u003e   0.444  expected  2.000\\nmps + s  -\\u003e  13.000  expected  6.000\\nmps - s  -\\u003e  -5.000  expected  2.000\\n```\\n\\nSo the 0-dim CPU-scalar promotion path appears to read the storage rather than the tensor's offset, and only for floating dtypes.\\n\\n**Why it matters in practice.** I found this from the other end: two schedulers in `huggingface/diffusers` return all-NaN output on MPS while being correct on CPU. Both keep their `alphas`/`betas` coefficient tables on the CPU and do `sample * self.betas[i]`, so from step 1 onward they silently multiply by the wrong coefficient and diverge into NaN.\\n\\n```python\\n# diffusers 0.40.0.dev0, same seed, same inputs\\nIPNDMScheduler           cpu: finite, mean -0.0586    mps: 4096/4096 NaN\\nKDPM2DiscreteScheduler   cpu: finite, mean -1.2485    mps: 4096/4096 NaN\\n```\\n\\nThe failure is silent end-to-end: a user on Apple Silicon gets a black image and no error. I plan to file the downstream issue on diffusers separately, since they can also defend against this by moving the coefficient tensors onto the sample's device — but the wrong arithmetic itself looks like it belongs here.\\n\\n### Versions\\n\\n```\\nPyTorch version: 2.13.0\\nIs debug build: False\\nOS: macOS-26.0.1-arm64-arm-64bit (Apple Silicon)\\nPython version: 3.11.14\\nMPS available: True\\nMPS built: True\\n```\\n\\n2.13.0 is the current release, so this is not a stale-version report.\\n\\n---\\n\\nDisclosure, per [AI_POLICY.md](https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/main/AI_POLICY.md): I used an AI coding assistant to help narrow this down, starting from the NaN outputs in diffusers and bisecting to the `storage_offset` condition. Every result above I ran myself on the machine described, and the reproduction is deliberately reduced to plain PyTorch with no third-party dependency so it can be checked in a few seconds. I have not looked at the MPS kernel source, so I have no proposed fix — the offset/dtype pattern is an observation from the outside.\\n\",\"closed_by_pull_requests\":{\"references\":[],\"total_count\":0},\"created_at\":\"2026-10-29T07:49:30Z\",\"has_children\":false,\"has_parent\":false,\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/Kineth-t/pytorch-triage-agent/issues/5\",\"labels\":[\"bot-triaged\",\"eval-background\",\"module: correctness (silent)\",\"module: mps\",\"triaged\"],\"number\":5,\"reactions\":{\"+1\":0,\"-1\":0,\"confused\":0,\"eyes\":0,\"heart\":0,\"hooray\":0,\"laugh\":0,\"rocket\":0,\"total_count\":0},\"state\":\"open\",\"title\":\"[MPS] Binary ops with a 0-dim CPU float tensor ignore storage_offset, silently using the wrong value\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-10-29T07:49:30Z\",\"user\":{\"id\":160092893,\"login\":\"Kineth-t\"}},\"method\":\"get\"}"}],"structuredContent":{"issue":{"assignees":[],"author_association":"OWNER","body":"### 🐛 Describe the bug\n\nOn MPS, a binary op between an MPS tensor and a **0-dim CPU float tensor with a non-zero storage offset** silently uses the value at storage offset 0 instead of the actual value. No error, no warning — just a wrong number.\n\n`coeffs[i]` for `i \u003e 0` is exactly this kind of tensor, so any code that keeps a table of coefficients on the CPU and scales an MPS tensor by one of them gets silently wrong arithmetic.\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\nmps = torch.full((2, 2), 4.0, device=\"mps\")\ncoeffs = torch.tensor([9.0, 2.0, 7.0])          # plain CPU tensor\n\nfor i in range(3):\n    s = coeffs[i]                                # 0-dim CPU view, storage_offset == i\n    got = (mps * s).flatten()[0].item()\n    print(f\"coeffs[{i}] = {float(s)}  offset={s.storage_offset()}  \"\n          f\"mps * coeffs[{i}] -\u003e {got}  expected {4.0 * float(s)}\")\n```\n\n```\ncoeffs[0] = 9.0  offset=0  mps * coeffs[0] -\u003e 36.0  expected 36.0\ncoeffs[1] = 2.0  offset=1  mps * coeffs[1] -\u003e 36.0  expected 8.0     \u003c-- wrong\ncoeffs[2] = 7.0  offset=2  mps * coeffs[2] -\u003e 36.0  expected 28.0    \u003c-- wrong\n```\n\nEvery result uses `coeffs[0]`.\n\n**Scope**, all measured on the same machine:\n\n| case | result |\n|---|---|\n| 0-dim CPU, `storage_offset == 0` | correct |\n| 0-dim CPU, `storage_offset != 0`, **float32 / float16** | **silently wrong** (reads offset 0) |\n| 0-dim CPU, `storage_offset != 0`, int32 / int64 | correct |\n| `coeffs[1].clone()` (offset reset to 0) | correct |\n| 1-dim CPU tensor, e.g. `coeffs[1:2]` | correctly raises `RuntimeError: Expected all tensors to be on the same device` |\n\nAll binary ops are affected, in both operand orders:\n\n```\nmps * s  -\u003e  36.000  expected  8.000\ns * mps  -\u003e  36.000  expected  8.000\nmps / s  -\u003e   0.444  expected  2.000\nmps + s  -\u003e  13.000  expected  6.000\nmps - s  -\u003e  -5.000  expected  2.000\n```\n\nSo the 0-dim CPU-scalar promotion path appears to read the storage rather than the tensor's offset, and only for floating dtypes.\n\n**Why it matters in practice.** I found this from the other end: two schedulers in `huggingface/diffusers` return all-NaN output on MPS while being correct on CPU. Both keep their `alphas`/`betas` coefficient tables on the CPU and do `sample * self.betas[i]`, so from step 1 onward they silently multiply by the wrong coefficient and diverge into NaN.\n\n```python\n# diffusers 0.40.0.dev0, same seed, same inputs\nIPNDMScheduler           cpu: finite, mean -0.0586    mps: 4096/4096 NaN\nKDPM2DiscreteScheduler   cpu: finite, mean -1.2485    mps: 4096/4096 NaN\n```\n\nThe failure is silent end-to-end: a user on Apple Silicon gets a black image and no error. I plan to file the downstream issue on diffusers separately, since they can also defend against this by moving the coefficient tensors onto the sample's device — but the wrong arithmetic itself looks like it belongs here.\n\n### Versions\n\n```\nPyTorch version: 2.13.0\nIs debug build: False\nOS: macOS-26.0.1-arm64-arm-64bit (Apple Silicon)\nPython version: 3.11.14\nMPS available: True\nMPS built: True\n```\n\n2.13....
